031 "Creating Exponential Growth Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050886 #edb/ref 1033 1499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050886 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6e409d61-3310-5c51-abad-da7413b800ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186144304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186144305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186144306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186144307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186144308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144303 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144303 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144303 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186144304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144303 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "2eb9ea82-bb25-57e8-9225-b8bc7341a2d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144304 #edb/ref 1080 "3859" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144304 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144304 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "907d6a8b-220d-5ffa-a86f-09363e7e2f9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144305 #edb/ref 1080 "3685" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144305 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144305 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "94778e61-ef88-5a09-9df7-8ee5df28bf58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144306 #edb/ref 1080 "1406" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144306 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144306 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ea98df17-9ef1-5762-bfe2-bd4e457076aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144307 #edb/ref 1080 "3754" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144307 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144307 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "430728e2-b29f-55c2-a057-2541cf308593" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144308 #edb/ref 1080 "1103" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144308 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144308 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "46941793-8685-5dbd-ab7e-f7fa10af6e94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147198 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1987" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147198 #edb/ref 1071 "A computer has $10$ files infected with a computer virus. If the number of infected files quadruples every hour, find a function that gives the number of infected files after $t$ hours. How many infected files will there be after $6$ hours?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147198 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e261e75-a3ec-59cf-a7b7-ad935cb4e55d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147198 #edb/ref 1074 "The number of infected files, $y(t),$ grows exponentially with a growth factor of $4.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 10$ is the initial number of infected files\n\n- $b = 4$ is the growth factor (the number of infected files quadruples)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in hours)\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny = ab^t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)= 10 \\cdot 4^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the number of files infected after $6$ hours, we  substitute $t=6$ into the above:\n\n$$\ny(6) = 10 \\cdot 4^{6} = 40\\,960\n$$\n\nTherefore, the computer will have $40\\,960$ infected files after $6$ hours." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147199 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1988" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147199 #edb/ref 1071 "In the week of opening, the grocery store sold $20$ kilograms of apples. After that, the weekly sales increased by $25 \\%$ every week. \n\nFind a function that models the total weight of apples sold in week $t$ after opening, and use this function to find the total amount of apples sold in the $4$th week." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147199 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d2d38b6b-8626-5605-835d-86370525d292" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147199 #edb/ref 1074 "The number of kilograms of apples sold $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $25 \\%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 20$ is the total weight of the apples (in kilograms) sold initially\n\n- $r = 0.25$ is the growth rate ($25 \\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in weeks) after opening\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny = a(1+r)^t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny   = 20 \\cdot (1 + 0.25)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\ny(t) = 20 \\cdot (1.25)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total weight of apples sold in week $4,$ we evaluate the above for $t=4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny(4) = 20 \\cdot (1.25)^{4} \\approx 49\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $49$ kilograms of apples were sold in week $4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147215 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21183" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147215 #edb/ref 1071 "A podcast was released with $80$ initial listeners. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each week after release, the number of listeners increased by $190\\%$ of the number of listeners at the end of the previous week.\n\nAccording to this model, how many listeners are there estimated to be after $2$ weeks?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147215 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "914dd92e-18b8-5583-8326-f534d1a112f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147215 #edb/ref 1074 "The number of listeners $L(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $190\\%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 80$ is the total number of listeners initially\n\n- $r = 1.9$ is the growth rate ($190\\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in weeks) after release\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nL = a(1+r)^t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $L(t)$ is\n\n$$\nL = 80 \\cdot (1 + 1.9)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nL(t) = 80 \\cdot (2.9)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of listeners after $2$ weeks, we evaluate the above for $t=2{:}$\n\n$$\nL(2) = 80 \\cdot (2.9)^{2} \\approx 673\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of listeners after $2$ weeks is $673.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147473 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147473 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147473 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147473 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147473 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147473 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147473 #edb/ref 1039 "Modeling Exponential Growth Using a Growth Factor" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147473 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186147198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147473 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "80c3fbf3-5165-510f-b130-d216d5c8aa19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147474 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147474 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147474 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147474 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147474 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147474 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147474 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147474 #edb/ref 1039 "Modeling Exponential Growth Using a Growth Rate" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147474 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186147199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147474 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1a494473-0da3-5789-a3da-08d8a0f62d6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147475 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147475 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147475 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147475 #edb/ref 1039 "Modeling Exponential Growth Using Percentage Increases Greater Than 100%" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147475 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186147215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147475 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bb2a01a6-39e9-55ac-ac4a-eaab72e72257" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147671 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147671 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147671 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147671 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147671 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147671 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147671 #edb/ref 1045 "Modeling Exponential Growth With Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147671 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186148356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147671 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147671 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147671 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b830a80c-d680-54e0-801a-894c774ffea4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147767 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186144303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147767 #edb/ref 1069 "q-318220" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147767 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147767 #edb/ref 1071 "A concert video was uploaded with $90$ initial views. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each hour after upload, the number of views increased by $250%$ of the number of views at the end of the previous hour.\n\nAccording to this model, how many views are there estimated to be after $3$ hours?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147767 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "66005c3f-ae8d-568e-8ad1-166663174f51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147767 #edb/ref 1074 "The number of views $V(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $250%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=90$ is the total number of views initially\n\n- $r=2.5$ is the growth rate ($250%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in hours) after upload\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nV=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $V(t)$ is\n\n$$\nV=90\\cdot (1+2.5)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nV(t)=90\\cdot (3.5)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of views after $3$ hours, we evaluate the above for $t=3:$\n\n$$\nV(3)=90\\cdot (3.5)^{3}≈3859\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of views after $3$ hours is $3859.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148356 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148356 #edb/ref 1061 531824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148356 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "642a7826-566a-5e56-b810-cb6fb7d5fa0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148356 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148357 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148357 #edb/ref 1061 531825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148357 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c0ba8192-ba62-514c-b119-d4b80274d19c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148357 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148358 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148358 #edb/ref 1061 531828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148358 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4ad684e1-3cee-5aab-8d3d-e3b084e25e37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148358 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148359 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148359 #edb/ref 1061 531829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148359 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c9b07cd7-55e0-5b22-8e5e-94373891336a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148359 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148360 #edb/ref 1061 531832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148360 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5e381067-3801-5773-b9a5-d5c897aa20dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148360 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148683 #edb/ref 1064 "When dealing with exponential growth, it's more common to be given a **growth rate** instead of a growth factor. A growth rate is usually expressed as a percent.\n\nFor example, suppose that an investor invests $\\[math]500$ dollars in the shares of some high-growth companies. It's expected that the investment will grow by $35\\%$ each year. Can we write a function that describes the value of the investment after $t$ years? \n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=\\[/math]500$ is the **initial value** of the investment\n\n- $r = 0.35$ is the **growth rate** (this is simply $35\\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t$ is the time (in years)\n\nLet $y(t)$ be the value of the investment after $t$ years. The formula for $y(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny = a(1+r)^t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our values for $a$ and $r,$ we get\n\n$$\ny(t) = 500(1+0.35)^t.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we arrive at\n\n$$\ny(t) = 500(1.35)^t.\n$$\n\nWhen creating exponential growth functions, the key takeaway is this:\n\n- If you're given a **growth rate** $r$ (usually expressed as a percent), then use the formula $y = a(1+r)^t.$\n\n- If you're given a **growth factor** $b,$ then use the formula $y = ab^t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148683 #edb/ref 1065 1936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148683 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "65e48fd4-d6c3-5e02-aad1-9a8dae46b0bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148683 #edb/ref 1067 "Modeling Exponential Growth Using a Growth Rate" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148684 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that there are $120$ deer in a wildlife reserve. If the deer population doubles every year, can we find a function that gives the number of deer after $t$ years? \n\nFirst, let's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 120$ is the **initial number** of deer\n\n- $b =2$ is the **growth factor** (equal to $2$ because the number of deer doubles every year)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in years)\n\nWe let $y(t)$ be the number of deer in the reserve after $t$ years.  The formula for $y(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = ab^t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $a$ and $b,$ we get\n\n$$\ny(t) = 120\\cdot 2^t.\n$$\n\nWe can use this formula to find the number of deer in the reserve for any value of $t \\geq 0.$ For example, to find the number of deer after $3$ years, we substitute $t=3$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(3) & =120\\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =120\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =960\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo there will be $960$ deer after $3$ years.\n\nFinally, notice that substituting $t=0$ into the formula gives the initial number of deer:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =120\\cdot {2}^{0} \\\\  & =120\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =120\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148684 #edb/ref 1065 1937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148684 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ed1b9eba-931a-5908-99c6-58b738afef7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148684 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488496 #edb/ref 1080 "y(t)=2\\,000+{2}^{t}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488496 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488496 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0c42295e-f712-5a56-90ec-b9b3ab0ee9c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488514 #edb/ref 1080 "y(t)=2\\,{000}^{t}+{2}^{t}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488514 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488514 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "14269be5-6f1d-50ca-9b8b-1b0e3e6814fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488545 #edb/ref 1080 "0.5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488545 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488545 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "247e479c-9bbc-5bbe-a0e3-0aae8ea1b13a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488582 #edb/ref 1080 "1506" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488582 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488582 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "360767c6-d66d-5903-9180-b0da4415a