17592186488928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489123 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bed8a07f-b1e3-5291-93c3-eed235b080d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489160 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489160 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489160 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489160 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489160 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489160 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489160 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489160 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186488621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489160 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f5d48576-5f2c-576e-8f97-f11d75b1ee78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489179 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489179 #edb/ref 1069 "q-318214" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489179 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489179 #edb/ref 1071 "An online course launched with $100$ initial students. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each week after launch, the number of students increased by $160%$ of the number of students at the end of the previous week.\n\nAccording to this model, how many students are there estimated to be after $3$ weeks?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489179 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0f781a71-78ec-54be-875d-95c07fb380d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489179 #edb/ref 1074 "The number of students $S(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $160%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=100$ is the total number of students initially\n\n- $r=1.6$ is the growth rate ($160%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in weeks) after the launch\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nS=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $S(t)$ is\n\n$$\nS=100\\cdot (1+1.6)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nS(t)=100\\cdot (2.6)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of students after $3$ weeks, we evaluate the above for $t=3:$\n\n$$\nS(3)=100\\cdot (2.6)^{3}≈1758\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of students after $3$ weeks is $1758.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489206 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489206 #edb/ref 1069 "q-61657" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489206 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489206 #edb/ref 1071 "A computer has $20$ files infected with a computer virus. The number of infected files $y(t)$ after $t$ days is given by the formula:\n\n$$\ny(t)=20\\cdot (\\text{___})^{t}\n$$\n\nIf the number of infected files increases $5%$ each day, then what is the missing value in the expression for $y?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489206 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3ef74434-74b6-5bd1-8d2d-3a97591fb1bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489206 #edb/ref 1074 "The number of infected files $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $5%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=20$ is the initial number of infected files.\n\n- $r=0.05$ is the growth rate ($5%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in days.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =20\\cdot (1+0.05)^{t} \\\\  & =20\\cdot (1.05)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $1.05.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489214 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489214 #edb/ref 1069 "q-63824" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489214 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489214 #edb/ref 1071 "At the beginning of a study, there were $2\\,000$ ants in an anthill. The study shows that the number of ants doubles every month. What function represents the number of ants $y(t)$ after $t$ months?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489214 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4d03e717-deaf-523f-a995-dad5a2ef0024" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489214 #edb/ref 1074 "The number of ants $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth factor of $2.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=2\\,000$ is the initial number of ants.\n\n- $b=2$ is the growth factor (the number of ants doubles every month).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in months.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=2\\,000\\cdot {2}^{t}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489252 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489252 #edb/ref 1069 "q-61866" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489252 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489252 #edb/ref 1071 "There are $14$ bacteria in a culture. Every minute, the number of bacteria increases by $15%.$ What function represents the number of bacteria $y(t)$ after $t$ minutes?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489252 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a1c43e4b-f0c3-5827-a9fe-03d9f0ad0749" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489252 #edb/ref 1074 "The number of bacteria $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $15%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=14$ is the initial number of bacteria.\n\n- $r=0.15$ is the growth rate ($15%$ expressed as a percent).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in minutes.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =14\\cdot (1+0.15)^{t} \\\\  & =14\\cdot {1.15}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489253 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489253 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103103" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489253 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489253 #edb/ref 1071 "The number of bacteria $y(t)$ in a culture after $t$ hours is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\ny=\\text{___}\\cdot {5}^{t}\n$$\n\nIf the culture started with $7$ bacteria and this number increases fivefold every hour, then what is the missing value in the expression for $y?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489253 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a3269c43-47df-5acc-97bf-5a6fd7c5c99e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489253 #edb/ref 1074 "The number of bacteria $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth factor of $5.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of the growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=7$ is the initial number of bacteria.\n\n- $b=5$ is the growth factor (the number of bacteria increases fivefold every hour).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in hours.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7\\cdot {5}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
